WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.360
Maybe Alien: Covenant wasn't quite the Alien
movie all the fans have been dying for.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.580
But it came pretty close!

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.700
It had lots of aliens, right?

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.830
As a bridge between Prometheus and the greater
Alien universe, Covenant bore a heavy burden

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:18.089
for fans of the franchise, and it took that
burden seriously.

00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:22.859
Love it or hate it, we got an official backstory
for the xenomorphs and the promise of more

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:24.300
mythology to come.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.029
What we also got was a ton of Easter eggs
pointing back to earlier Alien films.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:33.570
Here's a look, but be careful — some of
these eggs could have some pretty nasty contents

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.109
lurking inside.

00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:37.050
Like spoilers!

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.050
The bird

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.250
Die-hard fans were probably looking for this
lil' guy the whole movie, and they weren't

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.470
disappointed.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.289
This little drinking bird thing has been in
a few of the Alien movies.

00:00:47.289 --> 00:00:51.809
The first time you see it is the opening shot
of Alien, which has two of them alone on the

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:53.670
Nostromo's eating table.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:58.010
It shows up a few times throughout the movie,
particularly the scene where Kane takes an

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.649
alien to the chest.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:04.770
The same bird shows up in Aliens and then
again in the game Alien: Isolation.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.920
And now it's back again in Alien: Covenant.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:07.920
Why?

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:08.920
Who knows!

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.320
But at this point, that bird has been in the
franchise longer than Sigourney Weaver.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.480
The hidden trailer

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.250
Even before Covenant hit theaters, the marketing
team was already throwing out some Easter

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.250
eggs.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.610
One of those landed in the form of a brand
new teaser that featured this hashtag.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.360
Plug that into Instagram, and you would have
found an Instagram account with the same name

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.810
and only one post: a hidden trailer for Alien:
Covenant set to the tune of John Denver's

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.450
"Take Me Home, Country Roads" which Elizabeth
Shaw was singing in her transmission in the

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.450
movie.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.650
That's also the hashtag spelled backwards:
"take me home."

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.130
Terrible food

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:49.430
About a million people tuned into the extended
pre-release clip "Last Supper" on YouTube.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:54.480
In hindsight, releasing that scene was a beautiful
marketing move, since it's not even in the

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.480
movie.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:59.110
It's like an extra five minutes of movie for
all of us who've been carefully waiting for

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.890
each new Covenant teaser.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.770
And while that's kind of an Easter egg in
itself, we like to dig deeper.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.420
The entire scene is set up almost exactly
like the scene in Alien right before the first

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.970
chestburster pops out of Kane.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.280
Even the angle of the shot is almost identical.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.160
That's certainly not a coincidence, since
these are both Ridley Scott films.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.410
Then Upworth, played by Callie Hernandez,
starts choking in the last supper clip, and

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.650
Danny McBride's Tennessee makes this joke:

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.520
"Food's not that bad."

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.060
...which is almost exactly what Parker says
to Kane when he starts choking in the original

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.060
movie.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.660
"What's the matter?

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.840
The food ain't that bad, baby."

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.530
If there's one thing we've learned by now,
it's that when it comes to space, you should

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.260
probably pack your own lunch.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.940
"Bring some water!"

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.140
"Water, my ass!

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.460
Bring this man some Pepto Bismol!"

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.020
David and Walter

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:55.550
Michael Fassbender plays two parts in Alien:
Covenant: a reprisal of his David 8 role from

00:02:55.550 --> 00:03:00.670
Prometheus, and Walter, an updated version
of David that launched with the crew of the

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:01.670
Covenant.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:05.560
But for the longest time, the androids in
the Alien franchise seemed to be following

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.740
a Sesame Street naming pattern.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.630
Ash, Bishop, Call, David — they all went
in alphabetical order.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.790
Until...Walter.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:17.090
Not Eduardo, like we all expected, but Walter.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:22.180
Well, one fan theory behind that name choice
is as simple as it is logical: both of those

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.070
names are callbacks to David Giler and Walter
Hill, two producers who've been a part of

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.320
Alien since the very first film.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.320
Is it true?

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.430
It'd be an awesome tribute if it was.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.930
After all, these were the guys who rewrote
Dan O'Bannon's original script and basically

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.140
made the original sci-fi horror classic we
know and love.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.140
MU/TH/UR's Day

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.930
Ah, Mother, that disembodied voice that controlled
everything on the ship in Alien.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.020
Sometimes we dream about her counting down
the Nostromo's destruction.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:54.880
"In T minus 5 minutes."

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.300
"You bitch!"

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.940
While Mother bit the big one when the Nostromo
exploded, she's definitely back in prequel

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:04.959
form.

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:07.980
"Mother, how long have we been traveling?"

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.500
"Approximately 24 days."

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.450
Is it the same operating system that shows
up later — in film continuity — in Alien?

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.900
Alien: Covenant takes place about 20 years
before Alien.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.389
If so, Mother can't be very smart, because
she should have totally seen the events of

00:04:22.389 --> 00:04:26.060
Alien coming after everything that happened
in Covenant.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:27.060
Beerfest

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.300
Fun fact: astronauts love getting drunk.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.500
"It begins!

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.000
"Gimme that!

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:36.930
Stop it!"

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:41.639
In Alien, the crew kicks back with cans of
Aspen beer, a brew generously provided by

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:44.939
the company that wants to slay them all: Weyland-Yutani.

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:49.740
In Alien: Covenant, that beer is apparently
already part of the rations, because the crew

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.530
of the Covenant drinks the exact same beer.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:57.330
That's a cool, refreshing, frost-brewed throwback
to the film that launched the franchise.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.819
Paradise found

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:03.550
Back when names were still being tossed around
for Prometheus 2, one of the working titles

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:09.409
was Paradise Lost, a reference to John Milton's
17th-century poem about Satan's temptation

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:11.699
of Adam and Eve in the Garden of Eden.

00:05:11.699 --> 00:05:16.099
Alien: Covenant's name obviously changed in
the interim, but one of the lines in the movie

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:18.190
still alludes to Milton's poem.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:22.770
When David is fighting Walter, David asks,
"Would you rather serve in heaven or reign

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:23.770
in hell?"

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.689
In the context of the movie, he's basically
saying, "Quit being a sucky robot and slay

00:05:27.689 --> 00:05:28.940
everything with me."

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:33.599
But the line itself refers back to Book 1
of Milton's poem, when Lucifer says "Better

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:39.050
to reign in Hell, than serve in Heaven" right
after he gets tossed out of God's clubhouse.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:40.409
Writing on the wall

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:41.629
Let's talk facehuggers.

00:05:41.629 --> 00:05:46.599
Prometheus had none, but we all knew going
into Alien: Covenant that those little bastards

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:51.020
would be back in action, since Billy Crudup's
character Christopher took one to the face

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.129
in the trailer.

00:05:52.129 --> 00:05:55.689
But the question during the movie wasn't if
they'd show up, but when.

00:05:55.689 --> 00:05:59.860
And if you were paying attention, that moment
was basically spelled out right on the wall

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.490
of David's little Alien chop-shop.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:06.009
Right before David leads Christopher down
into the room where all the eggs are waiting,

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:10.419
they walk through a doorway with a hand-drawn
diagram of a facehugger stuck to the wall

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:11.729
right beside it.

00:06:11.729 --> 00:06:15.789
It was easy to miss — there were diagrams
plastered all over the walls — but if you

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:21.099
noticed it, it was pretty much a neon sign
foreshadowing Christopher's impending chest-bursty

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:22.099
ending.

00:06:22.099 --> 00:06:23.779
We missed you, you gross little cuties!

00:06:23.779 --> 00:06:25.530
Thanks for watching!

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:28.610
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.879
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

